1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:07,000
In 1979, the Arctic was loud.

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,000
48 years of sea ice. 48 maps. One chord per year.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Every September since 1979, the National Snow and Ice Data Center has mapped the Arctic.

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Each map becomes a chord. How much ice is left decides how many notes survive.

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,000
1979: six voices.

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:36,000
2012: three. The record low.

7
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,000
2026: four.

8
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Not a fade. A stagger.

9
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,000
You have never seen a satellite image.

10
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:55,000
You just heard forty-eight years of looking at the ice.

11
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,000
The ice is still two-thirds of what it was in 1979.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Now play it yourself.

13
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:04,000
1995 — still six voices.

14
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:06,000
2012 — three.

15
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,000
2026 — four.

16
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Four notes, held.

17
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Then one voice continues alone.

18
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:18,000
CODA — composition, not data.

19
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:20,000
The last measured chord is four.

20
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Icecho — The Last Note

21
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
Data: NSIDC Sea Ice Index v4 (G02135), 1979–2026.
NASA SVS 5677 credited.

22
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Built for the ear first.
